# ============================================================================
# Security 12 DHCP Alert - detect, LOG and BLOCK rogue DHCP servers.
# (This is DHCP SNOOPING - not the same as the built-in "/ip dhcp-server alert".)
#
# Snooping treats any DHCP *server* reply (OFFER / ACK / NAK) that arrives on an
# UNTRUSTED bridge port as a rogue server, and:
#     * BLOCKS it - dropped at the port, so a fake server can never hand a
#       poisoned address / gateway / DNS to your clients, and
#     * LOGS it (topic bridge,warning - verified on ROS 7.23), naming the PORT
#       and the MAC, e.g.
#         ether5: received DHCP server message on untrusted port
#                 from source IP 6.6.6.6, MAC DE:AD:BE:EF:00:02
#
# The router's OWN DHCP server is NOT affected: its offers are generated on the
# router, not received on a port, so snooping never blocks them (verified).
#
# READ BEFORE YOU PASTE
# 1. Trusted ports. Snooping blocks DHCP-server replies on EVERY port that is
#    untrusted (the default). So ANY bridge that legitimately RECEIVES DHCP
#    replies - an upstream/ISP server reached through a port, OR this router's
#    OWN WAN/mgmt lease if it runs a /ip dhcp-client on a bridge - needs that
#    one uplink port trusted, or the lease is dropped (a delayed outage / remote
#    lockout):
#        /interface bridge port set [find interface=<uplink>] trusted=yes
#    As a safety net the loop below SKIPS any bridge that itself carries a
#    /ip dhcp-client, so a bridged-WAN lease is not cut. If your dhcp-client
#    sits on a VLAN *over* the bridge, trust its uplink port by hand.
# 2. Performance. Enabling snooping turns OFF bridge fast-path (and hardware
#    offload on switch chips that don't support snooping) - a CPU/throughput
#    cost on that bridge. Fine for an edge/CPE; check /interface bridge before
#    enabling it on a high-throughput core.
# 3. Version. dhcp-snooping needs RouterOS >= 6.43; the MAC/IP detail in the log
#    is newer still (present on 7.x). On very old builds the command may error
#    or block-without-logging.
# 4. Scope. It only drops on the MikroTik bridge - a rogue and a victim sharing
#    the SAME dumb switch/AP behind one port still see each other. This is IPv4;
#    for IPv6 also set dhcpv6-snooping on the same bridges.
#
# Generalized: no bridge or port names are hard-coded - it enables snooping on
# whatever bridges exist (minus dhcp-client bridges). Safe on a router with one
# bridge, many, or none; re-run after you add a new bridge.
# ============================================================================

:foreach b in=[/interface bridge find] do={:local bn [/interface bridge get $b name]; :if ([:len [/ip dhcp-client find where interface=$bn]]=0) do={/interface bridge set $b dhcp-snooping=yes}}

# Optional - page yourself when a rogue appears. Uses a DEDICATED logging action
# (not the shared "remote" one, so existing syslog is untouched) and is guarded
# so re-pasting won't duplicate it. Set YOUR syslog IP, then uncomment all three:
#/system logging action add name=dhcpalert target=remote remote=YOUR-SYSLOG-IP
#:if ([:len [/system logging find where prefix="ROGUE-DHCP"]]=0) do={\
